@@ -2,41 +2,41 @@
 
 ## High
 
-### Pass 1 (frozen before repository access)
+- None found in Pass 1.
+
+## Medium
 
-- **`.svt-btn` still has a clipped, sub-44px effective target on desktop** `[Pass 1]`
-  - **Severity:** High
-  - **File:line:** `public/css/suite.css:2792`, `public/css/suite.css:2872`, `public/css/suite.css:2941`; `tests/desktop-and-css.spec.js:972`, `tests/desktop-and-css.spec.js:1352`
-  - **Triggering input or sequence:** At a viewport of 600px or wider, render the diff's own real-chain fixture, `.sheet-topbar > .svt-toggle > .svt-btn`, and tap above or below the button's roughly 24px visible box. The broad T2 selector `.sheet-topbar button::after` also matches `.svt-btn`, even though the T3 rationale says `.svt-btn` cannot use an overlay because `.svt-toggle` clips it with `overflow:hidden`. The T3 `min-height` is limited to `max-width:599px`.
-  - **Observable consequence:** On desktop, the nominal 44px pseudo-element is clipped to the small segmented-control box and the visible box is deliberately asserted to remain 24px high. Users therefore do not get the claimed 44px effective target. The 360px test passes via T3 box growth, while the desktop unchanged-size test measures only the border box and never verifies tappability; the desktop sidebar test is unrelated.
-  - **Confidence:** High. This follows directly from selector matching, the diff's explicit clipping statement, the media-query boundary, and the supplied fixtures/tests.
+### [Pass 1] An unrelated selector rewrite removes the downtime radio/checkbox text-colour rule
+
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `public/css/suite.css:1702`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: Render any downtime quick-form radio label, or a checkbox label under the normal single `#t-downtime` container. The diff removes the grouped `.qf-radio-label, .qf-checkbox-label` rule and replaces it with `#t-downtime #t-downtime .qf-checkbox-label`.
+- **Observable consequence**: The radio label no longer receives this scoped `color: var(--txt)` declaration at all. The checkbox declaration now requires one `#t-downtime` beneath another identically-ID'd ancestor, so it does not match the normal DOM either. Both controls can inherit a different colour, creating an unrelated visible regression outside the touch-target work.
+- **Confidence**: High. This follows directly from selector matching; runtime confirmation is deferred to Pass 2 because Pass 1 may inspect only the diff.
 
-## Medium
+### [Pass 1] “Real ancestor chain” tests replace each component subtree with synthetic markup
 
-### Pass 1 (frozen before repository access)
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `tests/desktop-and-css.spec.js:1217`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: Run either 360px `gdx3Measure` test. For every probe, the helper replaces the selected real tab's entire contents with a new inline-styled `#gdx3-host` and a hand-authored HTML fragment.
+- **Observable consequence**: Only the outer tab ancestors are real. Any production clipping ancestor, stacking context, positioning container, or adjacent control omitted from a probe's fragment cannot intercept or clip `elementFromPoint`, so an overlay can pass while failing in the live rendered component. The claim that the fixtures exercise each control's “real ancestor chain” is therefore overstated.
+- **Confidence**: High that the oracle is synthetic below the tab; Medium that a specific omitted ancestor causes a current false pass, pending repository tracing in Pass 2.
 
-- **An unrelated selector rewrite drops the downtime radio colour and makes the checkbox rule effectively non-matching** `[Pass 1]`
-  - **Severity:** Medium
-  - **File:line:** `public/css/suite.css:1699`
-  - **Triggering input or sequence:** Render `.qf-radio-label` or `.qf-checkbox-label` under the normal single `#t-downtime` container. The diff removes `#t-downtime .qf-radio-label` entirely and replaces the checkbox selector with `#t-downtime #t-downtime .qf-checkbox-label`, which requires two nested elements carrying the same ID.
-  - **Observable consequence:** Neither ordinary downtime radio labels nor checkbox labels receive the former `color:var(--txt)` declaration. Depending on inherited colour/theme, these controls visually regress; the change is also unrelated to touch-target sizing.
-  - **Confidence:** High for the cascade change; medium for its exact visual severity without repository context, which this pass was forbidden to inspect.
+### [Pass 1] Desktop-sidebar test never hit-tests the claimed effective area
 
-- **The desktop-sidebar test can report a 44px “hit area” without proving any of it is hit-testable** `[Pass 1]`
-  - **Severity:** Medium
-  - **File:line:** `tests/desktop-and-css.spec.js:1307`
-  - **Triggering input or sequence:** Give `.sidebar-app-tile::after` or `.sidebar-collapse-btn::after` a computed 44px box that is clipped by an ancestor, covered by another stacking context, or otherwise loses pointer hit testing. Run the added desktop-sidebar test.
-  - **Observable consequence:** The test reads pseudo-element computed width/height and accepts them, but unlike `gdx3Measure` it never calls `elementFromPoint`. It can therefore stay green while part of the claimed target cannot be clicked—the exact false-positive mode the diff's own main helper says computed size alone cannot exclude.
-  - **Confidence:** High that the test has this blind spot; whether current sidebar ancestors trigger it requires Pass 2 repository access.
+- **Severity**: Medium
+- **File:line**: `tests/desktop-and-css.spec.js:1306`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: Run `css-audit — the desktop sidebar chrome has a >=44px hit area`. The test appends synthetic controls to `#desktop-sidebar`, reads the element and `::after` computed sizes, and asserts those dimensions only.
+- **Observable consequence**: A 44px pseudo-element that is clipped, painted beneath another element, or otherwise not the `elementFromPoint` result still passes. This test therefore does not establish that `.sidebar-app-tile` and `.sidebar-collapse-btn` have a usable 44px hit area, unlike the stated rendered-hit-area strategy.
+- **Confidence**: High; there is no hit-test in this test body.
 
 ## Low
 
-### Pass 1 (frozen before repository access)
+### [Pass 1] Midpoint-only hit probes do not establish that the full overlay is unobstructed
 
-- **`.qf-radio-label{position:relative}` is dead touch-overlay scaffolding** `[Pass 1]`
-  - **Severity:** Low
-  - **File:line:** `public/css/suite.css:2795`
-  - **Triggering input or sequence:** Render `.qf-radio-label` at any width and inspect the new touch-target rules. It is included in the T2 “need position:relative” list but omitted from both pseudo-element overlay lists; at phone widths it instead receives T3 `min-height`.
-  - **Observable consequence:** The new positioning declaration serves no touch-target technique and creates an unnecessary containing block, increasing cascade/positioning risk without supplying a hit-area benefit.
-  - **Confidence:** High from the complete added selector lists in the diff.
+- **Severity**: Low
+- **File:line**: `tests/desktop-and-css.spec.js:1170`
+- **Triggering input or sequence**: Put an overlapping sibling or clipping shape over only a corner/partial edge of a 44px overlay while leaving its centre and four inset edge midpoints unobstructed, then run the tappability test.
+- **Observable consequence**: All five samples still resolve to the target and the test passes although part of the claimed rectangular hit area activates something else. This is most relevant for wrapped/dense layouts where neighbours can approach diagonally.
+- **Confidence**: High as a test-coverage limitation; Low-to-Medium that the current production layouts contain such a corner-only obstruction, pending Pass 2.
 

