161:   `.sh-ts-slot-add`, `.sh-ts-slot-btn`, `.rel-edit-btn`, `.rite-free-badge`, `.rite-xp-badge`).
162:   **This is the same carve-out boundary gdx-2 used**, for the same shared-stylesheet reason, with
163:   `.edit-tab` explicitly pulled out of it per the scoping call above. `.dot`'s specific
164:   row-pitch-overlap evidence is recorded there too.
165: 
166: ## Acceptance Criteria
167: 
168: 1. **Every selector in the in-scope inventory (the "Files to touch" table) has an effective hit area
169:    of at least 44px by 44px at a 360px viewport.** "Effective hit area" means the larger of the
170:    element's own `getBoundingClientRect()` and the used box of any hit-area pseudo-element on it, and
171:    that area must be genuinely tappable (the pseudo-element must **not** carry `pointer-events:none`).
172: 
173: 2. **No two in-scope hit areas overlap.** Expanding a hit area into a neighbouring control's hit area
174:    trades one mis-tap for another. Where the measured geometry does not allow 44px without overlap,
175:    the dev agent expands as far as the midpoint of the gap to the nearest interactive neighbour,
176:    records the selector and the achieved size in the Completion Notes, and does **not** claim AC1 for
177:    it. A short, evidenced exception list is an acceptable outcome; a silent overlap is not.
178: 
179: 3. **Desktop visual design is unchanged.** At viewport widths of 600px and above, every in-scope
180:    element's `getBoundingClientRect()`, and its computed `padding`, `border-width`, `font-size`,
181:    `background-color` and `border-radius`, are identical before and after this story. Technique T2
182:    satisfies this by construction; Technique T1 satisfies it only where the box is genuinely
183:    invisible; Technique T3 satisfies it because its rules live inside a `max-width` media query.
184: 
185: 4. **Phone visual design is unchanged too, except for an explicit, listed set.** The default is
186:    invisible expansion. Any selector that takes Technique T3 (a real box growth inside a phone-tier
187:    media query) is named in the Completion Notes with a one-line reason why T1 and T2 were both
188:    unsafe for it. An unlisted visual change on phone is an AC4 failure.
189: 
190: 5. **A `--tap-min` token is declared in `public/css/theme.css`'s `:root` block** and is used at every
191:    site this story touches, so the floor is greppable and the regression test has a hook:
192:    - `--tap-min: 44px;`
193:    It goes in `theme.css` because that is the declared token SSOT
194:    (`specs/architecture/coding-standards.md` -> CSS Standards, `specs/project-context.md` section 1),
195:    next to gdx-2's `--fs-floor-body` / `--fs-floor-micro` at `theme.css:180`. It is
196:    **theme-invariant**, so `:root` only, never duplicated into `[data-theme="dark"]`.
197:    **Declare it in `px`, deliberately, not `rem`.** gdx-2's floors are `rem` because text must scale
198:    with the OS text-size preference. A touch target is a physical finger, not text: it must not
199:    shrink when a user lowers their text size. Add a comment saying so, because the unit difference
200:    from its two neighbours otherwise looks like an oversight.
201: 
202: 6. **The three carve-outs (B1, B2, B3) are recorded in `specs/deferred-work.md`** with their measured
203:    selector lists and the reason for each, in the style that file already uses.
204: 
205: 7. **A checked-in regression test enforces AC1 and AC3 and ratchets against reintroduction.** See the
206:    Testing section for the required mechanism, the real-DOM mounting requirement, and the
207:    pre-existing-failure baseline.
208: 
